## The gitmattr manifesto

Most of the web doesn't need a database.

Your marketing site, your blog, your landing pages, your portfolio. None of these need a query language, a migration strategy, or a connection pool. They need files. Files that a human can read, edit, and understand without a manual.

Somewhere along the way, we started treating content management as an infrastructure problem. We built systems that require their own databases, their own APIs, their own deployment pipelines. We asked developers to adopt specific frameworks, specific folder structures, specific ways of thinking just to let someone update a paragraph.

I think that's backwards.

---

**A file is the most portable thing in software.** A Markdown file written today will be readable in thirty years. A YAML file doesn't care what framework renders it. You can move them between repos, between tools, between teams. No export. No migration. No vendor on the other end deciding what your content is worth.

**A CMS should adapt to your architecture, not the other way around.** gitmattr doesn't care if you use SvelteKit, Next.js, Astro, Hugo, or something that doesn't exist yet. If your framework reads files from a directory, gitmattr works. We don't generate your site. We don't own your build. We just give your team a way to edit what's already there.

**Collaboration doesn't require a platform.** It requires a good workflow. A client writes a draft. You review it. When it's ready, it ships. This is how publishing has worked for centuries. We just made it work with git.

**Your content lives in your repo.** Not in a database. Not behind APIs. Not locked into a schema. In your repo, in plain text, in files you control. If you stop using gitmattr tomorrow, your content doesn't move, because it never left.

---

I believe the best CMS is the one you eventually forget is there.